 "1900"] "\n" [:option {:value "33505"} "1904"] "\n" [:option {:value "33511"} "1906"] "\n" [:option {:value "33530"} "1912"] "\n" [:option {:value "33556"} "1920"] "\n" [:option {:value "33584"} "1924"] "\n" [:option {:value "33615"} "1928"] "\n" [:option {:value "33641"} "1936"] "\n" [:option {:value "33663"} "1948"] "\n" [:option {:value "33688"} "1952"] "\n" [:option {:value "33721"} "1956"] "\n" [:option {:value "33739"} "1960"] "\n" [:option {:value "33775"} "1964"] "\n" [:option {:value "33814"} "1968"] "\n" [:option {:value "33855"} "1972"] "\n" [:option {:value "33902"} "1976"] "\n" [:option {:value "33934"} "1980"] "\n" [:option {:value "33975"} "1984"] "\n" [:option {:value "34016"} "1988"] "\n" [:option {:value "34057"} "1992"] "\n" [:option {:value "34098"} "1996"] "\n" [:option {:value "34161"} "2000"] "\n" [:option {:value "34224"} "2004"] "\n" [:option {:value "259883"} "2008"] "\n" [:option {:value "312000"} "2012"] "\n" [:option {:value "359500"} "2016"] "\n" [:option {:value "19012000"} "2020"] "\n" [:option {:value "2002380"} "2024"]] [:script {} "$('#other_games_select').change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/303"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Football, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "19 – 24 October 1908"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/216"} "White City Stadium, London"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/33519"} "72 from 5 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Single-elimination tournament."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Hungary and Bohemia were the only world class soccer nations who did not take part in the 1908 Games. Their late withdrawal reduced the number of competing teams to six, and the " [:strong {} "Official Report"] " gives the reason for their absence as “political troubles in the Balkans”, though newspaper reports in their countries cite “financial reasons”.  This was a rather laconic way of describing the annexation of Bosnia and Herzegovina by Austria, an action which ultimately led to the outbreak of World War I six years later. It was suggested that the Austrian government put political pressure on them to withdraw, although the two nations were widely represented in other sports in London."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Similar to " [:a {:href "/results/35500"} "hockey"] ", each country was allowed to field four separate teams. This was seen as a way of encouraging the host nation to field teams for England, Scotland, Ireland and Wales. Membership of " [:a {:href "/organizations/7"} "FIFA (Fédération Internationale de Football Association)"] " was not required to enter the Olympic tournament - only that the nation had a functioning Football Association. France had left FIFA earlier in the year, but entered three teams and fielded two in London. Scotland, Ireland and Wales declined to participate, so England were left to carry British hopes alone. Denmark, the Netherlands and Sweden completed the entries."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The early rounds produced some high scoring matches and, with their " [:a {:href "/results/33524"} "17-1"] " defeat of France A, Denmark set an Olympic scoring record which has never beaten.  The Danish centre-forward, " [:a {:href "/athletes/24676"} "Sophus Nielsen"] ", also set an Olympic record by individually scoring 10 of the goals in Denmark’s overwhelming victory."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Before a crowd of 8,000, Great Britain met Denmark in the final.  In a classic match, Britain scored a goal in each half to give them a " [:a {:href "/results/33521"} "2-0"] " victory, but the Danes were highly praised for their skill and commitment.  In a playoff match for third place, the Netherlands beat Sweden " [:a {:href "/results/33522"} "2-0"] ".  Great Britain and Denmark maintained their pre-eminent position in world football, and met again in the " [:a {:href "/results/33532"} "1912 Olympic final"] ", when Britain were again the winners (4-2)."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Six Danes and two Britons played in both the 1908 and 1912 finals. As the Danish players " [:a {:href "/athletes/24706"} "Charles Buchwald"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/24673"} "Oscar Nielsen"] " had also played in the " [:a {:href "/results/33513"} "1906 final"] ", they became the only male players in history to take part in three Olympic football finals, as of 2024."] "\n"] [:button {:id "button739510", :class "btn btn-default"} "Details"] [:script {} "$('#button739510').click(splits)"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {}] [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Team"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Goals"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Yellow Cards"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Red Cards"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Penalty Kicks"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Shots"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Shots on Goal"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Corner Kicks"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Free Kicks"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Offsides"] [:th {:style "display: none", :class "split"} "Fouls Committed"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {:class "accordion-toggle", :data-toggle "collapse", :data-target ".team-members-470377"} [:div {:class "glyphicon glyphicon-plus"}]] [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} "Great Britain"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GBR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GBR.png"}] "GBR"]] [:td {:class "split", :style "display: none"} "18"] [:td {:class "split", :style "display: none"} "0"] [:td {:class "split", :style "display: none"} "0"] [:td {:class "split"